From 24630aea0f35d1e657fe592a9b971c022727e222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 14:31:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] An 8-bit HLG video is sent as Dolby
 Vision, as the same picture in 10 bits

"HLG as Dolby Vision" (videoscreen.hadeshlgasdv) took only 10-bit HLG: the
conversion to ST 2084, the measurement of the metadata and the RPU of
profile 8.1 (a base layer of 10 bits) are written for a 10-bit picture. An
8-bit HLG video (from a camera, or a broadcast in 8 bits) stayed HLG: with
"Automatic", where the output cannot send HLG, it lost its HDR and went
out as SDR; with "On" it played as HLG.

Now 8-bit 4:2:0 is eligible too (DVBRIDGE::HlgDolbyVisionEligible(),
CDVHdr10Plus::HlgMeasurable()), and the bridge is given each 8-bit picture
as the same picture in 10 bits: a pass on the GPU (dvbridge_gl_widen.c)
writes each code v of the NV12 planes of a VAAPI surface as (v << 2) << 6
in R16/RG16 textures, the planes of a P010 surface. Every step after it is
the 10-bit one, byte for byte: drawn from the 8-bit planes as they are,
libplacebo would normalise v / 255 with 8-bit range constants where the
10-bit picture is v10 / 1023 with 10-bit ones, the same values in exact
arithmetic but not the same floats, and the bridge refuses a base layer of
another depth than its RPU gives anyway. The pass reads each code with
texelFetch(), takes it back to the integer v and writes 256 v plus a
quarter of a step, so that the conversion to 16 bits gives 256 v whether
the GPU rounds or truncates; dithering is off for it, and the GL state of
the caller is put back.

Kodi's VAAPI renderer (with nvidia-vaapi-driver too) widens a picture once
(CRendererVAAPIGLES::WidenForBridge()): for the measurement ahead of its
drawing, the native output and the conversion, and anew for the next
picture of a buffer. A picture whose widening fails is not drawn by the
bridge, and kodi.log says so once. kodi.log says when a video's 8-bit
pictures are widened, and the line of the demuxer gives the depth.
10-bit HLG, HDR10, HDR10+ and Dolby Vision take no new step: the pass
runs only for an NV12 picture of HLG sent as Dolby Vision. A picture the
processor decodes is not measured at any depth (the measured route needs
VAAPI surfaces, as before), and 4:2:2 or 12-bit HLG stays HLG with the
reason in kodi.log.
